ion  okay pausing there for a second 10  billion dollars  well what's 10 billion dollars between  friends  there are reports this morning that  biden is about to unveil a six  trillion dollar budget six trillion  dollars  now as ben pointed out on twitter 6  trillion is actually 12 trillion when  you factor in the covet stimulus  his family's plan etc  that's all of the proposed spending in  just six months  he's proposing an average of 12 2  trillion dollars a month he has proposed  on average  by the end of his tenure at this rate  he'll have spent  i don't know what 96 trillion dollars if  my math is right which it probably isn't  either way the fact remains these are  astronomical amounts of money these are  such large amounts of money  that you can't even really call them  amounts of money  these numbers are so large they  effectively don't exist  there's no way to conceptualize them  biden is spending money as if there is  no such thing as money  i would say spending money like it grows  on trees but we need more trees than  exist  on earth to harvest this much so 10  billion dollars  that is pocket change i suppose but even  so it's a terrible plan for reasons that  should  already be clear but will only become  clearer as we keep reading  it says waters plan has triggered  concern in the mortgage industry that uh  because it would require lenders to  identify and direct aid to  first-generation buyers  those whose parents don't own homes and  by the way i have  no idea how they would even identify  that group and it does  and they don't really have any idea how  they would either there isn't some  um database out there as far as i know  of americans who've never bought homes  before  anyway and it would also direct the aid  to socially and economically  disadvantaged groups  the move is also stirring broader  criticism about how much it would help  buyers and whether it would even put  them at risk  we are simply providing first generation  home buyers large